A||||5||FieroRacer||12.5:1 Pistons||||10||
Z||000000||FieroRacer||10-03-2001||07:44 PM||||I saw on a website about some 12.5:1 compression Heavy Duty Pistons for the 2.8L.  Anyone every try these or have some advice.  I'm thinking of going with them for the rebuild.  Estimates show HP at about 176hp/206ft-lbs after the rebuild with just the pistons and a .010 rebore.||||reg||10
Z||000001||samoht||10-03-2001||08:29 PM||||i dont see how you could run that high of compression with the steal head design.  I did the 3.1 30 over and it is estamated at around 200 HP ...  good luck I wouldnt push past 10:1||||reg||1
Z||000002||Shaun41178||10-03-2001||09:36 PM||||you cuold run 101 octane and get away with it I am sure.  Just keep an ear out for pinging.  however that octane could get expensive.  What you could do is buy 93 at the pump and then add a gallon of toulene to a full tank.  10 gallons of gas and 1 gallon of toulene will put you around 103 octane or so.<P>I am sure you will need dyno tuning too with a Lambda meter stuck up your tailpipe to keep an eye on air/fuel ratios and I would also invest in an aftermarket knock meter to be on the safe side so you know if you have knock or not.  <P>The race engines back in the 80's used those high compression pistons to help push 270 hp out of the 2.8 but they were also big cammed and had a carb on it instead of tuned intake EFI like the fiero which is more of a torque producing intake.  I say go for it and see if you like it and let us all know.  Go with those pistons and a nice cam and some head work and you could have 200 hp or so.  Maybe.<P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR>Shaun41178@hotmail.com<BR>PORN FOREVER!||||reg||1
Z||000003||artherd||10-05-2001||07:27 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Shaun41178:<BR><B>... and had a carb on it instead of tuned intake EFI like the fiero which is more of a torque producing intake. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>HAH! That's putting it kindly!!!<P>270bhp from 2800cc ? impressive for a 2-valve motor, but childs play for any modern tuned production 4- or 5 valve powerplant.<P>I would be very afraid of running such a high compression withought a *FULL* fordged motor (including crank...) as well as an ECU with knock-sensor input and ascendant timing ajustment (read: DIS, read furthur, Electromotive, haltech, holley, etc)<P>Still, toss that in along with a lopey cam, and go to town! They make gas adatives (if youy don't mind roughly doubling your fueling costs, if you do you have the wrong car anyway  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> that will support that level of compression easially.<P>PS: polish the heads too, there will be no more rough edges for detonation to 'finger' off of. (it needs a 'grain' or 'seed' to start.)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||10-05-2001||04:47 PM||||I would go with the compression ratio that the camshaft manufacturer recommends for the camshaft you are going to use. 12-1 compression on a 2.8 with a stock or mild camshaft is not going to work very well!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroRacer||10-05-2001||06:24 PM||||I forgot about that factor.  I'll probably be talking with good ol daddy GM dealer monday to discuass options and price comparisons.  3.4, 3.8, 3.1, 2.8 HD, etc...<P>I've also decided to probably have them toss in a new tranmission, mounts, engine cradle bushings while they have the whole setup pulled.||||reg||1
